Table 3 - Empirical validation against known_data (Analysis C)
==============================================================

Pre-specified definitions (fixed before any comparison was computed):
  margin occupancy_pax          = 2.0
  margin travel_time_s          = 10.0
  margin trip_duration_s        = 60.0
  margin flow_per_visit_pax     = 0.5
  margin wait_median_s          = 30.0
  margin wait_tail_s            = 60.0
  margin prob_pp                = 2.0
  margin denied_rel             = 0.1
  margin headway_cv             = 0.05
  empirical acceptance: Bhattacharyya coefficient >= 0.97 (project rule, data_extractor.py)
  empirical W1 tolerance: 2.0 passengers
  operational issue rule (D5): P(O >= 60) >= 0.10
  crowding threshold: 60 passengers (75 % of capacity 80)
  peak slots: (7, 8, 9, 17, 18, 19)
  bootstrap: 1000 resamples of complete replicas, seed 20261013
  equivalence: 95 % CI of the IA-AG difference inside +-margin -> EQUIVALENT;
               CI entirely outside -> NOT EQUIVALENT; otherwise INCONCLUSIVE

Reference: known_data normal parameters, used as is. Not independent of the simulation inputs: the same
operational records calibrate both the inputs and this reference (calibration-period validation, plan 2.3).

C1 - Occupancy by direction x stop x trip-departure slot (475 strata, terminal visits excluded)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Distances are against the untruncated N(mu, sigma) of known_data. W1 in passengers, from CDFs on a 0.1 pax grid.
BC = Bhattacharyya coefficient with the project method (normal-normal if the simulated sample passes the
simplified normality test, else gaussian KDE vs normal); accepted if BC >= 0.97. W1 accepted if <= 2.0 pax.
P60 error = P_sim(O >= 60) - P_emp(O >= 60) in percentage points (empirical: normal tail with continuity correction).
